    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d983">

                <pb facs="1019" n="1005" xml:id="pg1005"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.8836/1228</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in Cuba</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Braden</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 6317</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Habana</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1944-03-15">March 15,
                        1944</date>.<lb/>[Received March 18.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>
                    <hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Supplementing the Embassy’s air mail
                    despatch No. 6158 of March 2, 1944 (File No. 815.6),<anchor n="66"
                        xml:id="d983-f1"/> in connection with the concessions of the American-owned
                    terminal and warehouse companies in Cuba, I have the honor to enclose, as of
                    possible interest to the Department, a copy and translation of the decision
                    handed down by the Habana Audiencia Court on February 22, 1944<anchor n="66"
                        xml:id="d983-f2"/> (not February 21 as erroneously stated in the Embassy’s
                    despatch No. 6158), suspending the application of that provision of Decree No. 2
                    of January 3, 1944, which prohibited the terminal companies from increasing
                    their tariffs in order to offset the 15 per cent wage increase which that decree
                    established.</p>
                <p>There are also enclosed a copy and translation of a letter dated March 1,
                        1944,<anchor n="66" xml:id="d983-f3"/> from Messrs. Lazo &amp; Cubas, the
                    attorneys for the United Fruit Company, commenting on the decision under
                    reference, which, in their opinion, has tended materially to strengthen the
                    terminal companies’ position vis-à-vis the Cuban Government.</p>
                <p>The Audiencia’s decision has been appealed by the prosecuting attorney. If, as
                    the attorneys for the terminal companies seem to expect, the Audiencia refuses
                    to admit the appeal, the companies, on posting a $10,000 bond, may proceed to
                    increase their tariffs within the limits set forth in their respective
                    concessions, pending a final decision in the matter by the Cuban Supreme
                    Court.</p>
                <p>Respectfully yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Ambassador: <lb/>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Albert</hi> F. <hi rendition="#small-caps"
                            >Nufer</hi>
                    </signed>
                    <lb/>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Counselor of Embassy for Economic Affairs</hi>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
